                                            <td class="description">      I am trying to publish an artifact to the shared repository but I am getting the following error:<br/>
<br/>
Caused by: d:\cpintegrationserver\modulepublicationserver\modules\vaultutil-3246a0a6-e364-4842-a6e8-1b7f5d957914\rbsfm\cpbuild\build\ivy-build-core.xml:509: impossible to publish artifacts for com.rbsfm#vaultutil;3: java.io.IOException: file copy not done from d:\cpintegrationserver\modulepublicationserver\modules\vaultutil-3246a0a6-e364-4842-a6e8-1b7f5d957914\temp\staging\vaultutil\lib\vaultutil.jar to \\lonms00804.fm.rbsgrp.net\d$\CPIVYREPOSITORY\cp-repository\com.rbsfm\vaultutil\3.part\vaultutil.jar: destination already exists and overwrite is false<br/>
<br/>
The full stack trace can be found at the bottom of this email. <br/>
<br/>
Now, I can confirm the following:<br/>
<br/>
a) the destination directory does NOT exist;<br/>
b) overwrite is set to true in the &lt;ivy:publish&gt; declaration (since I don&#39;t want any unwanted overwrites);<br/>
c) this behaviour is NOT encountered when publishing any other modules artifacts in our environment;<br/>
d) the module whose artifact is to be published has not been published before (it was created yesterday);<br/>
e) if I change overwrite=&quot;false&quot; it does publish the module artifact;<br/>
f) I&#39;ve tested this on our remote Ivy repository and on my local filesystem - both yield the same result;<br/>
<br/>
I can&#39;t emphasise enough that the destination directory does not exist. I&#39;ve tried it on three machines. Is the error message being generated misleading? Also, what is the &quot;.part&quot; directory for and why is it being created?<br/>
<br/>
Can someome help in this matter?<br/>
<br/>
Many thanks in anticipation,<br/>
<br/>
Stephen Mainstone<br/>
<br/>
==========================================================================================<br/>
