                <tr id="issuerow12831105" rel="12831105" data-issuekey="IVY-1522" class="issuerow">
                                            <td class="project">    Ivy
</td>
                                            <td class="issuekey">

    <a class="issue-link" data-issue-key="IVY-1522" href="https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/IVY-1522">IVY-1522</a>
</td>
                                            <td class="summary"><p>
                Ivy 2.4.0 improperly handles modules with colon (:) in version
    </p>
</td>
                                            <td class="issuetype"> Bug</td>
                                            <td class="status">
                Resolved
    </td>
                                            <td class="priority">   Critical
</td>
                                            <td class="resolution">    Fixed
</td>
                                            <td class="assignee">            Nicolas Lalevée
    </td>
                                            <td class="reporter">            Daniel Holmes
    </td>
                                            <td class="creator">            Daniel Holmes
    </td>
                                            <td class="created"> 19/May/15 18:03 </td>
                                            <td class="lastViewed"> &nbsp; </td>
                                            <td class="updated"> 26/May/17 10:31 </td>
                                            <td class="resolutiondate"> 26/May/17 10:31 </td>
                                            <td class="versions">                        2.4.0            </td>
                                            <td class="fixVersions">                        master            </td>
                                            <td class="components">                        Core            </td>
                                            <td class="duedate">    &nbsp;
</td>
                                            <td class="votes">0</td>
                                            <td class="watches">2</td>
                                            <td class="thumbnail"></td>
                                            <td class="timeoriginalestimate"></td>
                                            <td class="timeestimate"></td>
                                            <td class="timespent"></td>
                                            <td class="workratio">&nbsp;
</td>
                                            <td class="subtasks">                </td>
                                            <td class="issuelinks">                </td>
                                            <td class="environment"></td>
                                            <td class="description">      This was not an issue in 2.2.0.<br/>
<br/>
I have some modules in my project which produce debian .deb files and those modules versions include a colon &#39;:&#39; in the version because the version scheme was reset, so an epoch is set.<br/>
<br/>
Also for this module the ivy.xml declaration uses extends tag, and in processing the parent location, this code in FileUtil which is called during a FileUtil.normalize() is causing the : in the resolve parent file name to have the &#39;:&#39; replaced with a &#39;:/&#39;.<br/>
<br/>
The comment on the it seems to explain that it is trying to handle a Windows/NetWare case, but the assumption to process any colon in the path is too broad and is negatively affecting my case which is executing on a linux system.  Even on windows, this would only be attempting to address a drive letter case, right, where colon would occur only after a single letter in the path?<br/>
<br/>
   private static String[] dissect(String path) {<br/>
        char sep = File.separatorChar;<br/>
        path = path.replace(&#39;/&#39;, sep).replace(&#39;\\&#39;, sep);<br/>
<br/>
        // // make sure we are dealing with an absolute path<br/>
        // if (!isAbsolutePath(path)) {<br/>
        // throw new BuildException(path + &quot; is not an absolute path&quot;);<br/>
        // }<br/>
        String root = null;<br/>
        int colon = path.indexOf(&#39;:&#39;);<br/>
        if (colon &gt; 0) { // &amp;&amp; (ON_DOS || ON_NETWARE)) {<br/>
<br/>
            int next = colon + 1;<br/>
            root = path.substring(0, next);<br/>
            char[] ca = path.toCharArray();<br/>
            root += sep;<br/>
            // remove the initial separator; the root has it.<br/>
            next = (ca[next] == sep) ? next + 1 : next;<br/>
<br/>
            StringBuffer sbPath = new StringBuffer();<br/>
            // Eliminate consecutive slashes after the drive spec:<br/>
            for (int i = next; i &lt; ca.length; i++) {<br/>
                if (ca[i] != sep || ca[i - 1] != sep) {<br/>
                    sbPath.append(ca[i]);<br/>
                }<br/>
            }<br/>
            path = sbPath.toString();
  </td>
